description:
The three functions at the top stand for any system resource that must be opened and closed in pairs: a file, a network connection, a lock.

Write a class `Connection` that makes forgetting impossible:

- its constructor calls `openResource()` and remembers the id;
- its destructor calls `closeResource(id)`;
- `int id() const`;
- it cannot be copied (two objects would close the same id).

This is **RAII**. Whichever way the surrounding code leaves its scope (reaching the end, an early `return`, an exception), the destructor runs and the resource is released. The tests try all three.

starter.solution.cpp:
// A pretend resource: do not change these three functions.
inline int g_open{ 0 };
inline int g_nextId{ 1 };
inline int openResource() { ++g_open; return g_nextId++; }
inline void closeResource(int) { --g_open; }
inline int openCount() { return g_open; }

class Connection
{
public:

private:
    int m_id{ 0 };
};

solution.solution.cpp:
// A pretend resource: do not change these three functions.
inline int g_open{ 0 };
inline int g_nextId{ 1 };
inline int openResource() { ++g_open; return g_nextId++; }
inline void closeResource(int) { --g_open; }
inline int openCount() { return g_open; }

class Connection
{
public:
    Connection() : m_id{ openResource() } {}
    ~Connection() { closeResource(m_id); }

    Connection(const Connection&) = delete;
    Connection& operator=(const Connection&) = delete;

    int id() const { return m_id; }

private:
    int m_id{ 0 };
};

unitTests:
static_assert(!std::is_copy_constructible_v<Connection> && !std::is_copy_assignable_v<Connection>, "Connection must not be copyable");

bool earlyReturn(bool leave) { Connection c{}; if (leave) return false; return openCount() > 0; }
void throwing() { Connection c{}; throw std::runtime_error{ "something went wrong" }; }

TEST("open inside the scope, closed after it") { CHECK_EQ(openCount(), 0); { Connection c{}; CHECK_EQ(openCount(), 1); CHECK(c.id() > 0); } CHECK_EQ(openCount(), 0); }
TEST("closed on an early return") { earlyReturn(true); CHECK_EQ(openCount(), 0); }
TEST("closed when an exception flies past") { try { throwing(); } catch (const std::exception&) {} CHECK_EQ(openCount(), 0); }
TEST("several at once, each with its own id") { Connection a{}, b{}; CHECK(a.id() != b.id()); CHECK_EQ(openCount(), 2); }

